==========================================
CLOUD/DEVOPS DAY 05 NOTES — S3 Security Misconfigurations
Date: 27-11-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- A bucket policy with Principal:* + s3:GetObject + Resource:bucket/* makes every object publicly readable
- This exact pattern is behind most real-world "exposed S3 bucket" data breaches
- No AWS credentials are needed to exploit it - a plain curl/HTTP request is enough
- Removing the bucket policy immediately blocks the access again
- Real AWS has a separate "Block Public Access" setting as an extra safety net, independent of policies
- This is the auditor's mindset: look for Principal:* in bucket policies as the red flag

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] awslocal s3 mb + s3 cp (create bucket, upload file)
[ ] awslocal s3api put-bucket-policy (the misconfiguration)
[ ] curl http://localhost:4566/<bucket>/<file> (unauthenticated access test)
[ ] awslocal s3api delete-bucket-policy (the fix)
[ ] curl again to confirm access is blocked

paste your real output here :
$ curl http://localhost:4566/day05-sensitive-bucket/sensitive-data.txt   (BEFORE the fix)
> 

$ curl -i http://localhost:4566/day05-sensitive-bucket/sensitive-data.txt   (AFTER the fix)
> 


